Your body has never met this flu before, so it fights on two fronts: a fast general response within hours, and a targeted response that takes about a week to build. This first step brings in the cells involved and shows how the fast side passes the alarm to the targeted side.

```map
{"ops":[
{"op":"add","id":"root","parent":null,"kind":"question","title":"How immunity fights a new virus?","summary":"From first infection to lasting protection, traced on one flu case","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s1","parent":"root","kind":"question","title":"Who joins the fight?","summary":"The fast and slow defenders, and how the alarm passes between them","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s2","parent":"root","kind":"question","title":"How does the fight unfold?","summary":"What happens day by day until the virus is cleared","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s3","parent":"root","kind":"question","title":"Why is reinfection milder?","summary":"What stays behind after recovery, and why vaccines work","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s4","parent":"root","kind":"question","title":"What if helpers are lost?","summary":"How losing one cell type breaks the whole defence","status":"open"}
{"op":"model","id":"m1","step":"s1","mode":"build","title":"Flu, your first infection","case":"influenza A in your lungs, days 0-14, never met before","layer":"parts","frame":{"kind":"boxes","boxes":[{"id":"inv","label":"The invader"},{"id":"fast","label":"Fast first line, hours"},{"id":"slow","label":"Targeted line, days"}]},"verbs":["picked up by","shows to","activates","makes","prevents","causes","becomes","kind of"],"parts":[{"id":"virus","label":"Flu virus","role":"a virus that copies itself inside airway cells","accept":["influenza","flu","virus","influenza virus"],"slot":"inv"},{"id":"lungcell","label":"Infected lung cell","role":"an airway cell taken over as a virus factory","accept":["infected cell","lung cell","airway cell"],"slot":"inv"},{"id":"ifn","label":"Interferon","role":"an alarm protein released by infected cells","accept":["interferons","ifn"],"slot":"fast"},{"id":"dc","label":"Dendritic cell","role":"a scout cell that samples tissue and travels to lymph nodes","accept":["dendritic","dc","antigen presenting cell","apc"],"slot":"fast"},{"id":"helpert","label":"Helper T cell","role":"a coordinating white cell, also called CD4 T cell","accept":["helper t","cd4","cd4 t cell","t helper"],"slot":"slow"},{"id":"killert","label":"Killer T cell","role":"a white cell that destroys infected cells, also called CD8","accept":["killer t","cd8","cytotoxic t cell","ctl"],"slot":"slow"},{"id":"bcell","label":"B cell","role":"a white cell able to make one antibody shape","accept":["b cell","b lymphocyte"],"slot":"slow"},{"id":"antibody","label":"Antibody","role":"a Y-shaped protein that sticks to one viral shape","accept":["antibodies","immunoglobulin","ig"],"slot":"slow"}]}
{"op":"links","model":"m1","links":[{"id":"l1","from":"virus","to":"dc","rel":"picked up by"},{"id":"l2","from":"lungcell","to":"ifn","rel":"makes"},{"id":"l3","from":"ifn","to":"virus","rel":"prevents"},{"id":"l4","from":"dc","to":"helpert","rel":"shows to","key":true,"hint":"Which scout carries flu pieces to the lymph node for inspection?","reversed":"The scout shows virus pieces to the T cell; the T cell does not show them back.","ask":"Why can't the targeted line start on its own, without the scout?","rubric":"T cells only switch on when a cell presents the matching viral piece to them; without a presenter they never learn the target"},{"id":"l5","from":"helpert","to":"bcell","rel":"activates","key":true,"hint":"Which cell must give the go-ahead before antibody makers multiply?","reversed":"The helper T cell switches on the B cell, not the other way round.","ask":"Why does the antibody response need a second cell's signal?","rubric":"a B cell needs a helper T signal to multiply and make strong antibody; the double check avoids attacking the wrong target"},{"id":"l6","from":"helpert","to":"killert","rel":"activates"},{"id":"l7","from":"bcell","to":"antibody","rel":"makes","key":true,"hint":"Which cell produces the proteins that stick to the virus?","reversed":"Antibodies are made by B cells; they do not make cells.","ask":"Why do antibodies take about a week to appear on a first infection?","rubric":"the few B cells with the right shape must first be found, activated and multiplied before much antibody is made"},{"id":"l8","from":"antibody","to":"virus","rel":"prevents"}]}
{"op":"miscon","model":"m1","items":[{"id":"x1","claim":"Antibodies are there from day one","refute":"On a first infection, useful antibody takes about 1-2 weeks; the fast line holds the virus back until then."}]}
{"op":"predict","model":"m1","ask":"Which cell carries flu pieces to the lymph node and presents them to start the targeted line?","choices":[{"text":"Dendritic cell"},{"text":"Red blood cell","why":"Red blood cells carry oxygen and present nothing to T cells."},{"text":"Platelet","why":"Platelets help blood clot; they play no part in presenting virus."}],"answer":"Dendritic cell","hides":"l4"}
{"op":"whatif","model":"m1","fails":"dc","ask":"Your dendritic cells never reach the lymph node. Which parts never get switched on?","affected":["helpert","killert","bcell","antibody"],"why":"T cells only start when a presenting cell shows them the virus, and B cells wait on T cells."}
]}
```

This step follows a first flu infection day by day, from the first infected airway cells to the virus being cleared around day 14.

```map
