These steps follow how Dijkstra works, starting with what it keeps track of while it searches. The first step uses a small set of Yorkshire roads, with round example distances, to show what each piece is for.

```map
{"ops":[
{"op":"add","id":"dijkstra","parent":null,"kind":"question","title":"How does Dijkstra find shortest paths?","summary":"The greedy method that settles the nearest town first","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s-parts","parent":"dijkstra","kind":"question","title":"What does Dijkstra keep track of?","summary":"The tables and lists it keeps while searching","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s-round","parent":"dijkstra","kind":"question","title":"How does one round run?","summary":"Pick the nearest town, settle it, update its neighbours","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s-why","parent":"dijkstra","kind":"question","title":"Why is a settled distance final?","summary":"Why settling the nearest town first gives the right answer","status":"open"}
{"op":"add","id":"s-neg","parent":"dijkstra","kind":"question","title":"When does Dijkstra give wrong answers?","summary":"What one negative road does to the method","status":"open"}
{"op":"model","id":"m1","step":"s-parts","mode":"build","title":"Leeds to York by road","case":"Leeds, Wakefield, Harrogate, York; example road lengths 15-45 km","layer":"parts","frame":{"kind":"boxes","boxes":[{"id":"in","label":"Given"},{"id":"work","label":"Kept while searching"},{"id":"out","label":"Result"}]},"verbs":["sends to","then","reads","contains","part of","lowers","makes","becomes","enables"],"parts":[{"id":"start","label":"Leeds (start)","role":"the town the search begins from, at distance 0","slot":"in","accept":["leeds","start","source"]},{"id":"roads","label":"Road lengths","role":"example km on each road, e.g. Leeds–Wakefield 15","slot":"in","accept":["road lengths","edge weights","weights","road costs"]},{"id":"dist","label":"Distance table","role":"best km found so far for every town","slot":"work","accept":["distance table","distances","dist"]},{"id":"pq","label":"Priority queue","role":"unsettled towns, smallest distance out first","slot":"work","accept":["priority queue","min heap","heap","queue"]},{"id":"settled","label":"Settled towns","role":"towns whose distance can no longer change","slot":"work","accept":["settled towns","settled set","visited","done set"]},{"id":"prev","label":"Previous town","role":"for each town, the town it was reached from","slot":"work","accept":["previous town","previous","predecessor","parent pointer"]},{"id":"route","label":"Shortest route","role":"the chain of towns from Leeds to York","slot":"out","accept":["shortest route","shortest path","path"]}]}
{"op":"links","model":"m1","links":[{"id":"l1","from":"start","to":"pq","rel":"sends to"},{"id":"l2","from":"roads","to":"dist","rel":"lowers","key":true,"hint":"When a shorter way to Wakefield turns up, what changes its number?","reversed":"The table never changes the roads; the road lengths are what bring a town's number down.","ask":"Why can a town's number only go down, never up?","rubric":"a distance is only replaced when a road gives a smaller total; a longer way is ignored"},{"id":"l3","from":"dist","to":"pq","rel":"sends to"},{"id":"l4","from":"pq","to":"settled","rel":"sends to","key":true,"hint":"Where does the town with the smallest distance go once it is taken out?","reversed":"Towns leave the queue to be settled; settled towns never go back in.","ask":"Why is the town with the smallest distance the safe one to settle?","rubric":"with no negative roads, any other way to it passes a town at least as far, so nothing shorter can turn up"},{"id":"l5","from":"prev","to":"route","rel":"makes","key":true,"hint":"How do you get the list of towns, not just the km to York?","reversed":"The route is read off the previous-town entries, starting at York and going back.","ask":"Why does storing one previous town per town give the whole route?","rubric":"following them back from York to Leeds gives the chain of towns; each one is the last step of a shortest path"}]}
{"op":"miscon","model":"m1","items":[{"id":"x1","claim":"A town is final as soon as it is first reached","refute":"York first gets 40 km via Harrogate, then 60 → only final when it is the smallest in the queue; a later road can still lower it.","link":{"from":"dist","to":"settled","rel":"sends to"}}]}
{"op":"predict","model":"m1","ask":"The queue hands out the town with the smallest distance. Where does that town go?","choices":[{"text":"Settled towns"},{"text":"A first-seen list","miscon":"x1"},{"text":"The back of the queue","why":"A town taken out is never queued again; its distance is done."}],"answer":"Settled towns","hides":"l4"}
{"op":"whatif","model":"m1","fails":"pq","ask":"You swap the priority queue for a plain first-in, first-out line. Which parts can now be wrong?","affected":["settled","prev","route"],"why":"Towns get settled in arrival order, not by distance, so a longer distance can be locked in too early."}
]}
```

